    "prompt": "You are extracting the numeric claims of one section of a model-validation report, so that each\ncan be checked against the artifact it cites. Section: outcomes.\n\nThe prose is printed below one line per line, each prefixed with its own number and a vertical\nbar. List EVERY number that appears in the prose, in the order it appears, including numbers that\nappear inside a citation's logical name and numbers written with a thousands separator or a per\ncent sign. For each one:\n\n- \"line\" is the number printed to the left of the line the number appears on. Give the number\n  only; do not copy the line's text back, and do not count the prefix itself as a number.\n- \"value\" is the number as written, with thousands separators and the per cent sign removed:\n  \"3,500\" is 3500, \"22.0%\" is 22.0.\n- \"unit\" is \"percent\" when the number is written with a per cent sign, \"count\" for a number of\n  rows, features or events, \"currency\", \"bp\" for basis points, \"months\" for a horizon, and\n  \"ratio\" for every other dimensionless quantity (AUC, PSI, VIF, a coefficient, a lift).\n- \"metric\" is what the number measures, taken from the logical name of the artifact it cites\n  (auc, brier, psi, vif, calibration_slope, delta_auc, coefficient, event_rate, ...), or null.\n- \"split\" is train, test, out_of_time or vintage_holdout, or null.\n- \"comparison\" is \"eq\" for a number that equals its artifact; \"delta\" for a difference written\n  \"from a to b\" or \"a gap of x\", whose value is the second cited artifact minus the first; and\n  \"ratio\" for a number written as one artifact divided by another. A delta or ratio carries TWO\n  adjacent citations, in that order.\n- \"citation\" is the [[art:...]] token the number carries, copied exactly, or null when the\n  sentence carries none. Do not invent a citation and do not move one from another number.\n- \"rounding\" is the number of decimals the prose wrote when it wrote fewer than the artifact has\n  (2 for a lift written 2.24), or null.\n\nReturn every number even when it has no citation: a number you leave out is still counted, and\ncounted against the report.\n\nProse:\n 1| **Discrimination.** AUC is 0.7883 in-sample and 0.7753 / 0.7718 / 0.7846 on test, vintage holdout and out-of-time respectively (Gini 0.577 / 0.551 / 0.544 / 0.569; KS 0.456 / 0.416 / 0.452 / 0.432). The largest train-to-holdout AUC gap is 0.0165 (train to vintage holdout), with 0.0131 to test and 0.0037 to out-of-time. All are well inside any conventional degradation tolerance, and the out-of-time result is especially reassuring: discrimination is essentially preserved through a regime in which the event rate doubled. **No out-of-sample degradation defect is raised.** This conclusion must, however, be read against Section 3 — because the vintage holdout shares loans with training, its gap is a lower bound on true cohort-transfer degradation.\n 2| \n 3| **Calibration.** Results are mixed and split-dependent.\n 4| \n 5| - `train`: slope 0.9937, mean predicted 0.008427 versus observed 0.008525 (−1.1%); CPR 9.66% predicted versus 9.76% actual. Excellent, as expected in-sample.\n 6| - `out_of_time`: slope 0.9969, mean predicted 0.018866 versus observed 0.017949 (+5.1%); CPR 20.43% versus 19.53% actual (+4.6%). Good, and notable given the regime shift.\n 7| - `test`: slope 0.9365, mean predicted 0.008116 versus observed 0.008061 (+0.7%); CPR 9.32% versus 9.26%. Aggregate level is nearly exact; the slope below 0.95 indicates mild over-dispersion of scores — the model spreads risk slightly too far from the mean — but it sits at the edge of, rather than outside, a conventional 0.90–1.10 band.\n 8| - `vintage_holdout`: slope 0.8373, mean predicted 0.005647 versus observed 0.004817 (+17.2%); CPR 6.57% predicted versus 5.63% actual (+16.7%). This breaches a 0.90–1.10 slope band and represents a material aggregate over-prediction.\n 9| \n10| The vintage-holdout result is the economically significant one. A 16.7% relative over-statement of CPR on a low-prepayment cohort translates directly into understated MSR value and understated duration, and the direction is systematic rather than noise: the low slope means the over-prediction is concentrated in the high-score tail, i.e. the model overstates prepayment most for the loans it ranks as most likely to prepay. Brier (0.004763) and log loss (0.02811) are lowest on this split, but that is an artifact of the low base rate and carries no calibration information.\n11| \n12| **Reconciliation checks.** Every split's reported CPR reconciles to its mean predicted monthly hazard under 1−(1−h)^12 to within rounding (e.g. 1−(1−0.018866)^12 = 0.2043 versus a reported 0.20432; 1−(1−0.005647)^12 = 0.0657 versus 0.06570), and each actual CPR reconciles identically to its split event rate. The metrics file [[art:31ce3d08:run.metrics]] is internally consistent and consistent with [[art:dca79f2f:run.splits]] on row counts and event rates. No arithmetic or reporting defects were found.\n13| \n14| **Effective challenge.** No challenger or benchmark model was registered. There is no logistic baseline, no constant-hazard or table-driven baseline, and no prior model version against which the 0.7883 AUC or the hazard specification can be judged. Without a challenger, validation cannot demonstrate that the spline baseline and the twelve-covariate specification add value over a simpler alternative, and the benchmarking element of effective challenge is unmet.\n\n\nAnswer with one JSON object and nothing else: no prose before or after it, no explanation, no markdown fence. It must validate against this JSON schema:\n{\"$defs\": {\"Comparison\": {\"description\": \"What the claimed value is asserted to equal.\\n\\n``delta`` and ``ratio`` are cited by two adjacent ``[[art:...]]`` tokens, in order (D-012).\\nThe enum is not nullable: ``eq`` is the explicit default, so \\\"this number equals its artifact\\\"\\nhas one spelling and not two (D-018).\\n\\nAttributes:\\n    eq: The value matches the cited artifact.\\n    delta: The value matches ``second - first``.\\n    ratio: The value matches ``second / first``.\", \"enum\": [\"eq\", \"delta\", \"ratio\"], \"title\": \"Comparison\", \"type\": \"string\"}, \"ExtractedClaim\": {\"additionalProperties\": false, \"description\": \"One claim as the model returns it: the grammar minus the fields the caller already knows.\\n\\n``text``, ``section``, ``id`` and ``source`` are not asked for. The caller knows which section\\nit passed in, the id is a hash of the other three fields, the source of an extraction is\\nalways ``report``, and the text is the line the model named -- which the caller has in front\\nof it, so asking for it back costs output tokens and adds a way for the answer to disagree\\nwith the prose (D-063, D-085).\\n\\nAttributes:\\n    line: The 1-based number of the line the number appears on, as the prompt printed it.\\n    value: The number as written, separators and per cent sign removed.\\n    unit: How it is written.\\n    metric: What it measures, or ``None``.\\n    split: Which split it is about, or ``None``.\\n    comparison: ``eq``, ``delta`` or ``ratio``.\\n    citation: The citation the number carries, or ``None``.\\n    rounding: Decimals the prose used, or ``None``.\", \"properties\": {\"line\": {\"minimum\": 1, \"title\": \"Line\", \"type\": \"integer\"}, \"value\": {\"title\": \"Value\", \"type\": \"number\"}, \"unit\": {\"$ref\": \"#/$defs/Unit\", \"default\": \"ratio\"}, \"metric\": {\"anyOf\": [{\"type\": \"string\"}, {\"type\": \"null\"}], \"default\": null, \"title\": \"Metric\"}, \"split\": {\"anyOf\": [{\"$ref\": \"#/$defs/SplitName\"}, {\"type\": \"null\"}], \"default\": null}, \"comparison\": {\"$ref\": \"#/$defs/Comparison\", \"default\": \"eq\"}, \"citation\": {\"anyOf\": [{\"type\": \"string\"}, {\"type\": \"null\"}], \"default\": null, \"title\": \"Citation\"}, \"rounding\": {\"anyOf\": [{\"minimum\": 0, \"type\": \"integer\"}, {\"type\": \"null\"}], \"default\": null, \"title\": \"Rounding\"}}, \"required\": [\"line\", \"value\"], \"title\": \"ExtractedClaim\", \"type\": \"object\"}, \"SplitName\": {\"description\": \"The splits a claim may be stated on, exactly as ``CLAIMS_SCHEMA.json`` enumerates them.\\n\\nAttributes:\\n    train: The development split.\\n    test: The held-out split.\\n    out_of_time: A later-period split; hazard subjects.\\n    vintage_holdout: One origination year held out; hazard subjects.\", \"enum\": [\"train\", \"test\", \"out_of_time\", \"vintage_holdout\"], \"title\": \"SplitName\", \"type\": \"string\"}, \"Unit\": {\"description\": \"How a number is written, which decides how it is normalised before matching.\\n\\nAttributes:\\n    ratio: Every dimensionless quantity not written with a ``%`` -- AUC, PSI, VIF, a\\n        coefficient, a lift.\\n    percent: Written with a ``%``; normalised against a unit-interval artifact by dividing\\n        by 100.\\n    count: A number of rows, features or events. Matched to half a unit, so integers match\\n        exactly.\\n    currency: A money amount.\\n    bp: Basis points, as a rate shock is written.\\n    months: A horizon.\", \"enum\": [\"ratio\", \"percent\", \"count\", \"currency\", \"bp\", \"months\"], \"title\": \"Unit\", \"type\": \"string\"}}, \"additionalProperties\": false, \"description\": \"The JSON object the extractor is asked for: a list of claims under one key.\\n\\nA bare JSON array is a shape more models get wrong than a single-key object, and\\n:func:`~quaestor.llm.structured.structured` validates a ``BaseModel``, not a list.\\n\\nAttributes:\\n    claims: The claims, in the order the numbers appear.\", \"properties\": {\"claims\": {\"items\": {\"$ref\": \"#/$defs/ExtractedClaim\"}, \"title\": \"Claims\", \"type\": \"array\"}}, \"title\": \"ExtractedClaims\", \"type\": \"object\"}",
    "system": null
  },
